<p class="heritage-longread__dek">An eight-generation story, from a Gaelic surname on the cold edge of Donegal &mdash; Ó Dochartaigh &mdash; through the coal-and-iron belt of industrial Lanarkshire, across the Atlantic, and down at last into a small Alberta town called Alix and a city called Calgary. It is one of the oldest of the lines that meet in Thomas Cheesman&rsquo;s children, and one of the hardest to see clearly, because for most of its length it was written down by no one who loved the people in it.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-the-thin-thread"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Prologue</span>The thin thread</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Ireland → Scotland → Alberta · c. 1750 to today</p><p>Some family lines come down to us thick with paper. There are baptismal registers in careful clerical hand, parish marriage entries, gravestones, wills, photographs with names pencilled on the back. You can almost hear those ancestors breathing.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>They tholed. And they came through.</p></aside><p>The Docherty line is not like that. For long stretches of it &mdash; the Irish years, the early Scottish years &mdash; there is almost nothing. No baptism we can read. No photograph. No letter. The people who carried this name were, for most of two centuries, exactly the kind of people that history does not write down: the poor, the Irish, the labourers in the coal pits and the iron foundries and the cotton mills, the ones who arrived with nothing and were counted, when they were counted at all, only as a number in a census column or a name misspelled by a clerk who had never heard it before.</p><p>So the most honest thing that can be said at the start is this: most of this story rests on what the family itself remembered and wrote into its own tree, not on records pulled from the archives. The two documents that would confirm its spine &mdash; the death certificate of an old Irishman who died in a Renfrewshire weaving town in 1862, and that of his son in a Lanarkshire coal town three years later &mdash; exist, in principle, in a building in Edinburgh. They have not yet been read. Until they are, the Irish-and-Scottish half of this story stands on a thread: probable and inherited, but not yet <em>proven</em>.</p><p>So it is told in two voices at once. One is warm and certain &mdash; because the <em>shape</em> of it is certain, the shape of the great Irish migration into industrial Scotland and then across the Atlantic to the prairie, a shape this family unmistakably belongs to. The other is careful, and keeps saying <em>probably</em>, and <em>the tree tells us</em>. Both are true at once, and a good family story does not hide one to make the other louder.</p><p>There is a word in the Lowland Scots this family would have spoken in the streets of Johnstone and Hamilton and Ayr long before they ever saw Canada, and the word is <em>thole</em>. <em>To thole</em> is to bear a thing under load without breaking; to endure a weight that has no end in sight, the way you endure a long winter or a hard year underground. It is a heavier word than <em>suffer</em>, and a more stubborn one than <em>survive</em>. It is the truest word for this family, because the Dochertys were never the loud kind of survivors. They carried what came &mdash; the crossing, the coal, the prairie, the buried children &mdash; and did not break under it. They tholed. And they came through.</p><p>This is their story, told as truly as the thin thread allows.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-john-and-mary-the-deepest-dark-c-1750s"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter One</span>John (and Mary) &mdash; the deepest dark, c. 1750s</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">c. 1750s · the cold edge of Donegal</p><p>At the very bottom of the tree, below everything else, there are two names with no dates beside them at all: <strong>John Docherty</strong> and a woman recorded only as <strong>Mary</strong>. They are listed as the parents of the man who opens the documented part of this story, John Edward Docherty, born in 1780. If that link is right, then John and Mary would have been born somewhere around the 1750s &mdash; the speculative floor of this family&rsquo;s memory. We do not know where they lived, what they did, or even whether they existed as named here rather than a tree-builder&rsquo;s reasonable guess at &ldquo;John Edward&rsquo;s parents must have been called something.&rdquo;</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>They are the dark water this whole family rises from.</p></aside><p>But we can say what their world almost certainly was, because every signpost points one direction. The surname Docherty &mdash; and all its spellings, Doherty, Dougherty, Dougharty, the clerks never agreed &mdash; comes from the Irish Ó Dochartaigh, &ldquo;descendant of Dochartach,&rdquo; a name meaning something like <em>the hurtful one</em> or <em>the obstructive one</em>. And the Ó Dochartaigh were not scattered evenly across Ireland. They were overwhelmingly a clan of one place: the Inishowen peninsula, the great wild thumb of land in the far north of County Donegal, jutting up between Lough Swilly and Lough Foyle into the cold North Atlantic. The last great chief of Inishowen, Sir Cahir O&rsquo;Doherty, led the final rebellion of Gaelic Ulster in 1608 and was killed for it, and after him the old order broke: the lands were planted with English and Scottish settlers, and the Gaelic lords became, over a few hard generations, Gaelic tenants, and then Gaelic poor.</p><p>So when we say John and Mary were <em>probably</em> Inishowen people, we are reading the surname like a fingerprint &mdash; a strong likelihood, not a proof. No Donegal register reaches back far enough to hold their baptisms; the earliest Inishowen registers begin only in the 1830s and 1840s, two and three generations too late.</p><p>We can know the <em>kind</em> of place, though the family&rsquo;s own religion in these earliest Irish years is genuinely uncertain. Late-eighteenth-century Inishowen was a hard, contested corner of Ireland, layered by the long aftermath of the Plantation of Ulster. A Donegal family carrying a Gaelic surname might by then be Catholic, Protestant of Scots descent, or Gaelic stock that had conformed to a Protestant church &mdash; and family memory, with a Protestant bible Robert Docherty&rsquo;s mother gave him in 1908, tells us that within a few generations this was a firmly Protestant family. What they certainly were, at the root, was <em>poor</em> and <em>of the land</em>: tenants and labourers scratching potatoes and oats out of soil that gave grudgingly, on the rocky, rain-flayed ground of Inishowen.</p><p>This is the floor the family stands on. Two people, John and Mary, on the cold edge of Ireland, poor in a country that gave its poor very little, speaking Irish as readily as English. Everything that comes after &mdash; the crossing to Scotland, the pits, the prairie, the small Alberta town &mdash; is the long story of their descendants climbing up off that floor. What they made of their own hard lives, whether they reckoned themselves ill-used or simply bent to the day&rsquo;s work and the next, no one thought to write down, and no one now can know. We owe them the acknowledgement even though we cannot see their faces. They are the dark water this whole family rises from.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-john-edward-docherty-1780-1862-the-man-who-cross"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Two</span>John Edward Docherty (1780&ndash;1862) &mdash; the man who crossed the water</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1780&ndash;1862 · Donegal → Johnstone, Renfrewshire</p><p>With <strong>John Edward Docherty</strong>, the thread becomes something we can almost hold. He was born, the family record says, in 1780, in Ireland &mdash; and he died in 1862 in Johnstone, Renfrewshire, Scotland. Eighty-two years, and somewhere in the middle of them, a crossing.</p><p>Think about what 1780 to 1862 contained for an Irishman. He was born into a country dangerously dependent on one crop and bound ever more tightly to a Britain it had not chosen. He would have been a young man during the 1798 Rising, crushed with a brutality that scarred Ulster for a generation; he lived through the Act of Union of 1801, which abolished the Irish Parliament; and he was around sixty when the Great Famine came in 1845 &mdash; <em>An Gorta Mór</em>, the Great Hunger &mdash; when the potato blight rotted the one food the Irish poor depended on, a million people died, a million more fled, and Ireland&rsquo;s population fell by a quarter and never recovered.</p><p>But by the Famine years, John Edward was already gone. We know this because he died in Scotland, and his wife&rsquo;s family, the McGeoghans, were settled in Renfrewshire by the 1780s. Which raises one of this story&rsquo;s real unresolved questions: <em>who was the immigrant?</em> Did John Edward cross from Ireland as a young man and marry into a McGeoghan family already established in Scotland, or had the Dochertys themselves been in Renfrewshire longer than we think? We do not yet know. The single document that could settle it &mdash; his 1862 Johnstone death certificate, which under Scottish law would name <em>his</em> parents and perhaps the Irish townland they came from &mdash; sits unread, the most valuable unpulled record in this whole branch.</p><p>That timing may be why the line survived at all. Inishowen &mdash; poor, crowded, potato-dependent &mdash; was the kind of place the Famine fell hardest on; whatever Dochertys remained there when John Edward had gone, it came for them. The family that reached Canada did so in part because its leading edge had already crossed the water before the worst of it.</p><p>For John Edward was part of one of the great population movements of the age. Even <em>before</em> the Famine, the Irish were crossing the narrow water to industrial Scotland in their tens of thousands: a short, cheap passage from Derry or Belfast to Glasgow or Greenock, into mills and pits and ironworks hungry for cheap labour. Most of that migration was Catholic, and built the despised, separate Irish-Catholic enclaves of the Clyde. But a substantial Protestant Irish stream crossed in the same boats, much of it from Ulster, and it had a markedly different experience: sharing the Presbyterian faith of the country it came to, it could blend into Lowland Scottish life rather than into a walled-off slum. This is the stream the Dochertys belong to, and their relatively smooth assimilation &mdash; Scottish-born children within a generation, marriage to Scotswomen, an unobstructed path onward to Canada &mdash; is itself a quiet sign of it.</p><p>He married <strong>Mary McGeoghan</strong>, also recorded as Nancy, born about 1786 in Renfrewshire, who died in 1856. The surname McGeoghan (Mag Eochagáin) is itself Irish, but a midlands name, from Westmeath and Offaly rather than Donegal &mdash; which deepens the puzzle of how a Donegal Docherty and a midlands-rooted McGeoghan family met and married in Lowland Scotland. It is the kind of mixing the great migration produced: Irish people from opposite ends of Ireland, thrown together in the Scottish industrial towns, marrying across distances they&rsquo;d never have crossed at home.</p><p>They settled in Johnstone, a weaving and milling town in Renfrewshire just west of Paisley &mdash; a planned industrial town of cotton mills, flax, thread, and coal pits opening in the surrounding fields, drawing exactly the labour John Edward had to sell. We do not yet know his trade; he may have been a weaver, a labourer, a collier. Mary died in 1856, John Edward six years later in 1862. They lie somewhere in Renfrewshire ground &mdash; an Irish couple who had crossed a sea and made a family stick in a new country, the first firm link in a chain that would reach, four generations later and half a world away, to a household on the Alberta prairie.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-peter-dougharty-c-1801-1865-the-coal-town-genera"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Three</span>Peter Dougharty (c. 1801&ndash;1865) &mdash; the coal-town generation</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">c. 1801&ndash;1865 · Ireland → the Lanarkshire coalfield</p><p>Here the spelling begins to slide. The son who carries the line forward is recorded as <strong>Peter Dougharty</strong> &mdash; <em>Dougharty</em>, not Docherty, because the clerks wrote down what they heard, and what they heard from an Irish mouth in a Scottish town they spelled a dozen different ways. Within this one family, in a single generation, the same name appears as Docherty, Dougharty, Doherty. We follow the spelling the records use and trust that the blood runs underneath it.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>A visitor wrote that it looked like the mouth of hell.</p></aside><p>Peter was born about 1801, in Ireland &mdash; which is itself a clue, and a complication. If John Edward was already in Scotland by the time he raised his family, why was Peter born in Ireland? Either John Edward came over later than the tree suggests, or the family moved back and forth across the water the way migrant labourers often did, following the work. There is also a tantalising, unconfirmed note in the tree: that Peter had a brother, Patrick Andrew Docherty, also born in Ireland around 1801, who died in 1871. <em>Twins?</em> The tree raises the question and cannot answer it.</p><p>Peter died on 3 October 1865, in Hamilton, Lanarkshire. And in that single line of fact &mdash; born in Ireland around 1801, died in Hamilton in 1865 &mdash; you can read the whole migration of his generation. He moved east, out of the Renfrewshire weaving country and deeper into Lanarkshire, which by the middle of the nineteenth century was the black, roaring engine room of the British Empire&rsquo;s iron and coal.</p><p>This is the world that made the next three generations of this family. The Lanarkshire coalfield and the ironworks along the Clyde valley &mdash; Hamilton, Bothwell, Bellshill, Coatbridge &mdash; were, in Peter&rsquo;s lifetime, among the most intense concentrations of heavy industry on earth. Coatbridge at night was said to glow red for miles; a visitor wrote that it looked like the mouth of hell. The pits went down hundreds of feet, and the work ate men: by roof-fall, by firedamp, by flooding, by the slow grinding of black lung that turned a strong man old before forty.</p><p>And the labour that did it was, in enormous part, Irish &mdash; emigrants with no capital but their bodies, pouring into the pits and foundries because that was the work on offer. The Catholic majority were resented for it &mdash; blamed for low wages and for the fevers that came through the <em>raws</em>, the long single-storey rows of company housing thrown up beside the pits, where cholera and typhus took the children first. The Protestant Irish, the Dochertys among them, lived in those same hard pit villages and did the same dangerous work, but carried one fewer mark against them, and slipped more readily into the Scottish working class. It was no soft life; the coal ate Protestant and Catholic alike. But their road through industrial Scotland ran a little straighter for it, which is part of why the family kept <em>moving</em>, out of Renfrewshire into Lanarkshire and at last across the Atlantic, rather than settling for good into a walled-off enclave.</p><p>Peter&rsquo;s wife is recorded as <strong>Mary Catherine</strong> &mdash; and here the family turns out to be more Scottish, already, than the surname alone would suggest. She was born on 8 November 1801 not in Ireland but in Govan, Glasgow; so too was Peter&rsquo;s own mother, Mary (or Nancy) McGeoghan, born in Renfrewshire in 1786. In this generation the Irish thread runs entirely through the <em>men</em>, John Edward and Peter, both Ireland-born, while the women they married were Scottish-born already. Mary Catherine is thought to have died about 1857 in Bellshill, one of the Lanarkshire coal villages, which places the family in the heart of the coalfield by the 1850s.</p><p>No document records what Peter did for his living, but the geography answers it almost as well as one would. A man born poor and Irish who moved his family into Hamilton and Bellshill in the 1840s and 1850s went there for one reason: the pits and the iron. He almost certainly spent his working life in or around the Lanarkshire mines and ironworks, and he died in Hamilton in 1865 having carried his family one long step deeper into industrial Scotland than his father had.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-thomas-dougharty-c-1835-1899-the-ayr-generation-"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Four</span>Thomas Dougharty (c. 1835&ndash;1899) &mdash; the Ayr generation, and the branch that broke for America</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">c. 1835&ndash;1899 · Ayr → Lanarkshire → Mercer County, Illinois</p><p>Peter&rsquo;s son <strong>Thomas Dougharty</strong> was born about 1 January 1835 in Ayr, on the Ayrshire coast southwest of Glasgow &mdash; and with him, for the first time in this story, we have a Docherty born <em>in Scotland.</em> The family that crossed the water as Irish poor was becoming, generation by generation, Scottish: Scots-born, Scots-speaking, Scots in everything but the surname and the still-fresh memory of Ireland.</p><p>Thomas was one of several children, with a brother, James Dougharty (born 1831&ndash;32 in Ayr), and a sister, Margaret (born 1838 in Ayr). Ayr in the 1830s was a county town and seaport &mdash; coal, weaving, harbour trade &mdash; and the Doughartys were there as part of the same restless following of work that had moved them out of Ireland and across Scotland. That the children were born in Ayr while the next generation surfaces in the Hamilton/Bothwell coalfield tells us the family kept circulating through the industrial belt, wherever a man could get taken on.</p><p>Thomas married <strong>Katherine Brown</strong>, born in March 1835 in Faskine, Lanarkshire &mdash; a colliery district near Airdrie and Coatbridge. Katherine was, in other words, a coalfield woman, the daughter of the pits, and her marriage to Thomas is a marriage entirely inside the industrial Scotland this family then belonged to. But Thomas&rsquo;s life did not end in Scotland. Sometime in the 1890s he and Katherine joined the great migration across the Atlantic &mdash; not to Canada but to the United States, to Mercer County, Illinois, where Dochertys were settling in the farm country south of Moline. Thomas died there on 13 April 1899 and was buried in Mercer County, a Lanarkshire collier laid to rest in American soil. It was this move, in the parents&rsquo; generation, that first carried the family out of Scotland, setting the stage for his son Robert&rsquo;s own journey on to the Canadian prairie.</p><p>And here, in Thomas&rsquo;s generation, the family does something it had not done before: it splits across an ocean.</p><p>Thomas&rsquo;s sister Margaret &mdash; Margaret Watson, born Dougharty in Ayr in 1838 &mdash; died not in Scotland but in Cable, Illinois. And around her a whole second Docherty world appears in the American records, living and dying through the late nineteenth and twentieth centuries in the small farming townships of Mercer County, southwest of the Mississippi mill cities of Moline and Rock Island.</p><p>This is the great fork of the Docherty story. One branch crossed to the American Midwest and became Illinois farmers; the central line stayed in Scotland one more generation and then leapt &mdash; not to Illinois, but to the Canadian prairie. And in one of those quiet coincidences family history throws up, the two would brush against each other again, because the woman the Canadian branch&rsquo;s patriarch would marry came from <em>exactly</em> the same corner of Illinois the American Dochertys had settled. But that is the next chapter&rsquo;s story.</p><p>There is an honest puzzle here too. The standard <em>1882 History of Mercer County, Illinois</em> contains not one mention of the Docherty name in any spelling &mdash; so either the family arrived after 1882 or it lived beneath the notice of the men who wrote county histories, as poor recent immigrants usually did. <em>Which</em> Docherty first made the leap, and when, is one of the genuine open questions of this line.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-robert-docherty-1872-1959-the-emigrant-the-homes"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Five</span>Robert Docherty (1872&ndash;1959) &mdash; the emigrant, the homestead, and the German-American widow</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1872&ndash;1959 · Hamilton → Illinois → Alberta</p><p>Now the story finds its hinge: the man who took this family out of Scotland for good and planted it in the Canadian West. He is the great-grandfather, <strong>Robert Docherty</strong>, and his is the first life in this whole line we can see in something like full colour, because it ends recently enough that records and family memory begin, finally, to overlap.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right heritage-longread__figure--rotated"><span class="heritage-longread__rot heritage-longread__rot--ccw"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_4532.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="Inscription in Robert Docherty's bible, dated 30 May 1908" loading="lazy" /></span><figcaption>Robert&rsquo;s bible, inscribed by his mother on his birthday &mdash; &ldquo;May 30th 1908.&rdquo; The family&rsquo;s proof of a Protestant line (and, below, passed &ldquo;To Lena, April 1, 1963&rdquo;).</figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>The reversal of two centuries of dispossession.</p></aside><p>Robert was born on 30 May 1872 in Hamilton, Lanarkshire &mdash; in the heart of the coal-and-iron country, into the same industrial Scotland that had held his father and grandfather. The tree records a whole crowd of siblings born across Bothwell and Hamilton through the 1850s, 60s, and 70s &mdash; Peter, Thomas, John, James, Catherine, Margaret, and Jane. A boy born in a Hamilton coal town in 1872 had a future mapped out for him before he could walk: down the pit, like his father and his father&rsquo;s father, for as long as his body held.</p><p>Robert did not take that future. He did what the boldest of his generation did &mdash; he left. He crossed the Atlantic, and instead of trading one industrial city for another, he went all the way out to the open prairie of central Alberta, to a small town called Alix &mdash; a railway-and-farming settlement east of Red Deer, on the parkland being thrown open to homesteaders in the years around 1900.</p><p>This was the great age of the prairie boom. After 1896 the Canadian government advertised the West across Britain and Europe as &ldquo;the Last Best West,&rdquo; the last great reserve of free farmland on the continent, and they came in their hundreds of thousands, the trains running west out of the eastern ports crammed with families and their crates. The pull was always the same: a hundred and sixty acres of your own, free but for a ten-dollar filing fee and the requirement that you live on it, build on it, and break a set acreage of sod within three years to &ldquo;prove up.&rdquo; It was advertised straight into the smoky industrial towns of Scotland, into places exactly like Hamilton, where a collier&rsquo;s son might read a railway poster and decide that the rest of his life did not have to look like his father&rsquo;s.</p><p>For a man whose family had been landless for as far back as anyone could trace &mdash; landless tenants in Ireland, landless labourers in Scotland, four generations of working other men&rsquo;s ground and other men&rsquo;s coal &mdash; the offer of a quarter-section of his <em>own</em> was the reversal of two centuries of dispossession. He took it.</p><p><em>Why Alberta &mdash; and why Alix?</em> Robert did not go straight from Scotland to the prairie. He went first to the United States, to Mercer County, Illinois, the farm country south of the Mississippi mill towns, where his own father Thomas Dougharty had already settled and died, in 1899, and where a cluster of Docherty kin had put down roots. It was there, among the Illinois Dochertys, that Robert met and married a German-American widow, and it was from Illinois, not Scotland, that he and his new blended family came north to Canada in 1912, living around Medicine Hat, Red Deer, and Lamerton before settling at last near Alix. The American branch of the last chapter was no side-branch at all: it was the family&rsquo;s own waystation, the middle leg of a Scotland → Illinois → Alberta migration.</p><p>Her name was <strong>Augusta Catherina Nitz</strong>, born on 24 September 1876 in Coal Valley, Rock Island County, Illinois &mdash; in the same corner of Illinois where Robert&rsquo;s own family had by then settled. She was the daughter of Herman Leopold Nitz and Lena (Kroeger) Nitz, German immigrants: the Nitzes out of Kreis Schwetz in what was then West Prussia, the Kroegers out of Puls in Schleswig-Holstein &mdash; part of the enormous German migration into the American Midwest. So the Docherty line, which had been Irish and then Scottish, now took in a German-American strain, and through Augusta the children of this marriage carried Prussian and Holstein blood alongside the Donegal and the Lanarkshire.</p><p>Augusta had been married before. Her first husband, whom she married at Moline in 1894, was Warren Martin Sullivan, and they had five children, the Sullivan half-siblings who appear all through the records: Warren Alfred, Martin Herman, Earl William, Emma Lovina, and Lena Elizabeth. Widowed, Augusta then married Robert Docherty on 11 July 1908, in Aledo, the county seat of Mercer County, Illinois. Out of that union came four Docherty children &mdash; Lena, Catharina Grace, Thomas Richard, and Abraham Lincoln &mdash; who grew up alongside their Sullivan half-siblings in one blended household that would, within a few years, cross the border into Alberta.</p><p>It is a very North American family that results, and you can read the whole optimism of the age in the names they gave their children. A daughter, Catharina Grace, born back in Illinois in 1909. And a son, born in Alix in 1917, given a name no Donegal tenant or Lanarkshire collier would ever have chosen, a name that announces a family pledging itself to a new country: Abraham Lincoln Docherty. From Ó Dochartaigh of Inishowen to a boy named for the American president, in five generations. That is the distance this family travelled.</p><p>Robert lived a long life on the prairie. He died on 31 August 1959 in Calgary, Alberta &mdash; eighty-seven years old, born in a Lanarkshire coal town when the telephone was still a dream and dying in a Canadian city in the year of the jet age. Augusta had gone before him, on 20 June 1944, in Alix, where she was buried in the Alix Cemetery. They are the bridge generation &mdash; the ones who carried this family across an ocean and a border and set it down, at last, on land of its own.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-thomas-richard-docherty-1914-1977-the-prairie-so"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Six</span>Thomas Richard Docherty (1914&ndash;1977) &mdash; the prairie son</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1914&ndash;1977 · Alix, Alberta · the prairie son</p><p>The boy through whom this line comes down to the present was <strong>Thomas Richard Docherty</strong>, born on 16 January 1914 in Alix, Alberta &mdash; a son of Robert and Augusta, born on the prairie, the first fully Canadian generation of this old Irish-Scottish family.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_3731-scaled.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="Elizabeth Annie (McIver) Docherty and Thomas Richard Docherty" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Granny and Grampa Docherty &mdash; Elizabeth Annie (McIver) and Thomas Richard.</figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>Two Gaelic dispossessions found each other in Alberta and made a home.</p></aside><p>His was the first Docherty life lived entirely in the new country. Where his father had crossed an ocean and his grandfather and great-grandfather had gone down the Lanarkshire pits, Thomas Richard was a child of the Alberta parkland &mdash; born the year the First World War broke out, a young man through the Great Depression and the Dust Bowl that scoured the prairie in the 1930s, when the rains failed and the topsoil blew away in black clouds and the homestead dream turned, for a great many families, to dust and debt. He came of age in the hardest decade the Canadian West has known, and he came through it.</p><p>And there was a streak in him the records only hint at. Through the 1930s, the worst of the Depression, Thomas Richard was a Golden Gloves boxer, taking prize fights for the purse when a purse was hard to come by; family memory holds that he once found himself stranded in California without money and fought his way home, one bout at a time. The boxing seems to have run in the blood: there is a long-held family tale &mdash; worth chasing before anyone swears to it &mdash; that his half-sister Lena Sullivan was a niece of John L. Sullivan, the last great bare-knuckle champion. And when the Second World War came, Thomas Richard served as an anti-aircraft gunner, stationed up the Pacific coast around Prince Rupert.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote"><p>He prize-fought his way back to Canada, one bout at a time.</p></aside><p>His life ran its full course in and around the town where it began. He married <strong>Elizabeth Annie McIver</strong>, and with that marriage the Docherty story braids together with another whole world &mdash; one that is a great story in its own right, and has its own telling. Elizabeth Annie was born on 20 March 1919 in Saltcoats, Saskatchewan, the daughter of a Hebridean family: McIvers of the Isle of Lewis and, through her mother Elizabeth &ldquo;Lizzie&rdquo; Campbell, Campbells of South Uist, Gaelic-speaking crofters cleared off the Scottish islands into a Saskatchewan colony scheme that failed by 1900. Through her, three more Scottish island lines &mdash; McIver, Campbell, and the Camerons of Moray behind them &mdash; flow into the family, and they carried their own grief: her uncle Norman George McIver, of Saltcoats, served with the 46th Battalion of the Canadian Expeditionary Force and was killed on 3 June 1917, aged twenty-six, and lies in a war cemetery in northern France. Here it is enough to say that when Thomas Richard married Elizabeth Annie, the Donegal-Irish Dochertys and the Gaelic-Hebridean McIvers became one family.</p><p>There is a quiet rhyme in that marriage. The Dochertys were Gaelic people from Ireland; the McIvers and Campbells were Gaelic people from the Scottish islands. Both came from the Gaelic edges of the British world, and both had been pushed off their ground &mdash; the Dochertys out of Donegal in the long aftermath of conquest, the Hebrideans off Lewis and South Uist by the Clearances that emptied the islands to make way for sheep. In Thomas Richard and Elizabeth Annie, two Gaelic dispossessions, one Irish, one Scottish-island, found each other in Alberta and made a home.</p><p>They raised their family in Alberta, the children clustered in the Calgary years: sons Donald Richard Docherty (born 1946), Roy Thomas Docherty (born 1951), David Norman Clark Docherty (born 1958) &mdash; and a daughter, the one through whom this line comes down to the present. Not every story is happy: Roy Thomas died young, on 30 August 1970 in Calgary, at only eighteen, a loss the family carried.</p><p>Thomas Richard himself died on 20 March 1977 in Alix &mdash; and there is something fitting in that. He was born in Alix in 1914 and he died in Alix in 1977, the prairie town his emigrant father had chosen bookending his whole life. His funeral was held at &ldquo;The Little Chapel on the Corner,&rdquo; with the Rev. J.L. Pottruff officiating. The Docherty journey, which had been all motion for five generations, had finally come to rest in one place long enough for a man to be born and to die in it. His grandfather had walked away from a Lanarkshire pit toward a quarter-section of his own; Thomas Richard lived out the life that quarter-section made possible.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-elizabeth-maryanne-docherty-b-1959-the-daughter-"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Seven</span>Elizabeth Maryanne Docherty (b. 1959) &mdash; the daughter who carries the name forward</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">b. 1959 · where the line turns toward the present</p><p>The Docherty line reaches the living present through <strong>Elizabeth Maryanne Docherty</strong>, born on 14 April 1959 in Canada &mdash; daughter of Thomas Richard Docherty and Elizabeth Annie McIver, and mother of Thomas Cheesman. Through her father she carries the Docherty inheritance; through her mother, the Hebridean &mdash; McIver of Lewis, Campbell of South Uist, Cameron of Moray.</p><p>Born in 1959, she is a child of the modern era &mdash; of postwar Alberta, of the oil years, of a Canada her homesteading grandparents would scarcely have recognised.</p><p>She spent most of her working life on her feet, serving &mdash; restaurant floors in whatever Alberta town the family was near, and later years cooking at an A&amp;W in Calgary before she set that down. These days she works the road-construction season as a flagger, her third or fourth year of it now, and the family teases her about the raccoon look the job leaves her with: tanned dark to the line of her sunglasses, a white band across the eyes. The life seems to suit her &mdash; little stress, lots of sun, the work optional rather than mandatory, and after a career spent serving truckers and equipment operators their coffee and their suppers, she gets along easily with the same men out on the road.</p><p>She also has a way of putting her family to work. At fourteen her son was bussing tables at Trumpeters, across from the Prairie Mall, because she was serving there and got him the job; in the Valleyview years she got him hired again, bussing and washing dishes through high school &mdash; at one memorably mad little restaurant where, by the end, four of the family were working at once, and where, one day, all four walked in together and handed over a note that said only <em>We Quit</em>, signed by each, and walked out. There is a fair argument that the whole cooking life told elsewhere on this site began with her.</p><p>And then the line does what every line in this family does: it does not run on alone. Her son, Thomas, would carry it forward &mdash; but not by itself, and not under its own name. His father was a Lakeman; the surname Thomas would carry through life, and pass to his own children, was neither Docherty nor Lakeman but Cheesman, taken from his step-father, Brian Cheesman. The final chapter is where the lines meet.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-where-the-docherty-river-joins-the-others"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">The Living Line</span>Where the Docherty river joins the others</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Calgary · Grande Prairie · the confluence</p><p>Every long family story has a moment when it stops being history and becomes the present. For the Dochertys, that moment is now.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>It has always, somehow, come through.</p></aside><section class="heritage-tree" aria-label="The Docherty family descent, nine generations from Inishowen to Grande Prairie"><h3 class="heritage-tree__title">The Docherty descent</h3><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">John Docherty</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Mary</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">c. 1750s · Ireland</span><span class="heritage-tree__children"><strong>John Edward</strong></span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Two names with <strong>no dates at all</strong> &mdash; the speculative floor of the family&rsquo;s memory, from the tree alone</li><li>The surname Ó Dochartaigh reads like a fingerprint: overwhelmingly a clan of <strong>Inishowen, Donegal</strong> &mdash; a strong likelihood, not a proof</li><li>Poor and of the land &mdash; tenants on the rocky, rain-flayed ground of Ireland&rsquo;s far north</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">John Edward Docherty</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Mary McGeoghan</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">Johnstone, Renfrewshire</span><span class="heritage-tree__children"><strong>Peter</strong><span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Patrick Andrew <em>(twins?)</em></span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Born in Ireland, 1780; died Johnstone, 1862 &mdash; <strong>the man who crossed the water</strong></li><li>Part of the pre-Famine Irish crossing into industrial Scotland, in the Protestant stream that could blend into Lowland life</li><li>Mary &mdash; also recorded <strong>Nancy</strong> &mdash; was Renfrewshire-born, 1786, of an Irish-midlands McGeoghan family</li><li>His <strong>1862 death certificate</strong>, unread at ScotlandsPeople, would name his parents &mdash; the most valuable unpulled record in the branch</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Peter Dougharty</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Mary Catherine</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">Hamilton · Bellshill</span><span class="heritage-tree__children"><strong>Thomas</strong><span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>James<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Margaret</span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>The spelling slides here &mdash; Docherty, <strong>Dougharty</strong>, Doherty &mdash; the clerks wrote what they heard</li><li>Born in Ireland c. 1801; died Hamilton, Lanarkshire, <strong>3 October 1865</strong>, deep in the coal-and-iron belt</li><li>Mary Catherine was <strong>Govan-born</strong>, 1801 &mdash; in this generation the Irish thread runs through the men alone</li><li>His 1865 Hamilton death certificate is the second key record still waiting to be read</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Thomas Dougharty</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Katherine Brown</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">Ayr → Illinois</span><span class="heritage-tree__children">Peter<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Thomas<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>John<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>James<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Catherine<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Margaret<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Jane<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span><strong>Robert</strong></span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>The first of this line <strong>born in Scotland</strong> &mdash; Ayr, c. 1 January 1835</li><li>Married <strong>Katherine Brown</strong> of Faskine, a daughter of the Lanarkshire coalfield</li><li>Took the family across the Atlantic in the 1890s &mdash; to <strong>Mercer County, Illinois</strong>, not Canada</li><li>Died 13 April 1899, buried in Mercer County &mdash; a Lanarkshire collier laid to rest in American soil</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Robert Docherty</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Augusta Catherina Nitz</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">m. Aledo, Illinois 1908</span><span class="heritage-tree__children">Lena<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Catharina Grace<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span><strong>Thomas Richard</strong><span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Abraham Lincoln</span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Born Hamilton <strong>30 May 1872</strong>; walked away from the pits, through Illinois, to the prairie at <strong>Alix, Alberta</strong></li><li>Married the widowed Augusta at Aledo, <strong>11 July 1908</strong> &mdash; she brought five Sullivan children to the blended household</li><li>The family crossed into Canada in <strong>1912</strong> &mdash; the Illinois years and the crossing now FamilySearch-corroborated</li><li>Augusta died at Alix, 1944; Robert in Calgary, <strong>1959</strong>, aged eighty-seven &mdash; the bridge generation</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Thomas Richard Docherty</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Elizabeth Annie McIver</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">Alix, Alberta</span><span class="heritage-tree__children">Donald Richard<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Roy Thomas<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>David Norman Clark<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span><strong>Elizabeth Maryanne</strong></span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Born <strong>16 January 1914</strong> in Alix and died there in 1977 &mdash; the first fully Canadian generation</li><li>A <strong>Golden Gloves boxer</strong> through the Depression &mdash; family memory says he prize-fought his way home from California</li><li>An <strong>anti-aircraft gunner</strong> at Prince Rupert in the Second World War</li><li>Married <strong>Elizabeth Annie McIver</strong> &mdash; the Hebridean McIver, Campbell, and Cameron lines join here</li><li>Roy Thomas was lost young &mdash; Calgary, 1970, at only eighteen</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Elizabeth Maryanne</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">b. 1959</span><span class="heritage-tree__children"><strong>Thomas</strong><span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Christopher<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Jonathan</span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>The youngest of the four &mdash; Docherty through her father, the Hebridean lines through her mother</li><li>A serving life: restaurant floors across Alberta, later an A&amp;W kitchen in Calgary; now she flags the road-construction season</li><li>Got her son his first restaurant job at fourteen &mdash; there&rsquo;s a fair argument the whole cooking life began with her</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Thomas</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Melanie</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">m. 2016</span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Married <strong>2 July 2016</strong> in Calgary &mdash; the city where Robert&rsquo;s road had ended is where theirs began</li><li>The parents of Patience, Daniel, and Faith</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Patience · Daniel · Faith</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">Grande Prairie</span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Born 2013, 2015, and 2017, all in Grande Prairie</li><li>Heirs of all eight family lines &mdash; the confluence this whole site is built around</li></ul></div></div></div></section><p>The Docherty line comes down through Elizabeth Maryanne Docherty to her son, <strong>Thomas Cheesman</strong>, born in Calgary on the 4th of November. In Thomas, the two halves of his own blood meet: the Lakeman line through his father, Martin Gerard Lakeman, and the Docherty line &mdash; together with the McIver, Campbell, and Cameron Hebridean lines carried through his grandmother Elizabeth Annie &mdash; through his mother. Calgary, where his great-grandfather Robert had died, is where they met.</p><p>But Thomas is not the end of the line, and the Dochertys are not a line at all in the old sense &mdash; not a single thread narrowing to one male heir. Thomas and <strong>Melanie Haiste</strong>, who married on the 2nd of July, 2016, in Calgary, and who are the parents of three children, pass everything they carry, jointly, to those three: to <strong>Patience Kristie-Anne Cheesman</strong>, born in Grande Prairie in 2013; to <strong>Daniel Eric Thomas Cheesman</strong>, born in Grande Prairie in 2015; and to <strong>Faith Lana Lyn Cheesman</strong>, born in Grande Prairie in 2017.</p><p>The three of them inherit it together. Not one as &ldquo;the heir&rdquo; and the others as branches off the side &mdash; all three, equally, as a sibling-group into whose keeping the whole of it is handed.</p><p>And what is handed to them is not the Docherty line alone. It is one of several inheritances now gathered in a single household: the Lakeman line, out of the Dutch polders and the Indies, and the Docherty and Hebridean McIver-Campbell-Cameron lines, all reaching the children through their father; and through their mother Melanie, the Haiste line out of the Yorkshire tanneries and the Rycroft line (with Steinke beside it) out of industrial Leeds by way of Hawai&rsquo;i. And there is the Cheesman name itself &mdash; given not by blood but by belonging, the name Thomas took from his step-father and gave to his children. Five inheritances. Two parents. Three children, in a house in Grande Prairie, carrying the whole of it forward.</p><p>None of the people in this story could have imagined them. John and Mary, on the cold edge of Donegal in the 1750s, landless and poor on the rocky ground of Inishowen, could not have imagined a great-great-great-great-great-granddaughter named Faith being born free in a prairie country that did not exist. Robert Docherty, walking away from a Hamilton colliery toward a quarter-section of Alberta sod, was reaching for exactly this and could not have known its faces.</p><p>The Dochertys tholed. They bore the hungry years and the pits and the crossing and the buried children, and did not break under the bearing.</p><p>That word is theirs to inherit now. Patience, Daniel, and Faith inherit it together &mdash; <em>thole</em>, and <em>come through</em> &mdash; along with everything else this family pours into them. The world they will live through is not yet written. But the family has been here before. It came through Donegal and the crossing of the Irish Sea. It came through the Lanarkshire dark and the long climb out of it. It came through the Atlantic and the Dust Bowl and the long labour of breaking new ground. It has always, somehow, come through.</p><p>It will again.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-lineage-at-a-glance">Lineage at a glance</h2><p>A quick-reference summary of the direct line, oldest to newest. The <strong>Confidence</strong> column shows how firmly each generation rests on a primary record versus the family&rsquo;s own tree.</p><div class="heritage-longread__ribbon" aria-hidden="true"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">John &amp; Mary</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">c. 1750s</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">John Edward</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1780&ndash;1862</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Peter</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">c. 1801&ndash;1865</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Thomas</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">c. 1835&ndash;1899</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Robert</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1872&ndash;1959</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Thomas Richard</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1914&ndash;1977</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbo