</table></div><p class="heritage-longread__aside"><em>Side-branches named in the records but off the direct line: Peter&rsquo;s possible twin brother <strong>Patrick Andrew Docherty</strong> (b. ~1801 Ireland, d. 1871); Thomas&rsquo;s siblings <strong>James</strong> (b. 1831&ndash;32, Ayr) and <strong>Margaret Watson née Dougharty</strong> (b. 1838 Ayr, d. Cable, Illinois) &mdash; the doorway to the American branch; the <strong>Mercer County, Illinois Dochertys</strong> (Thomas B., John Kenneth, Jean Alice, James Henderson); Robert&rsquo;s many Bothwell/Hamilton-born siblings; the <strong>Sullivan</strong> half-siblings Augusta brought into the household; Thomas Richard&rsquo;s brother <strong>Abraham Lincoln Docherty</strong> and his Alix-country descendants; and Thomas Richard&rsquo;s own children <strong>Donald Richard</strong> (&ldquo;Uncle Rick&rdquo;), <strong>Roy Thomas</strong> (1951&ndash;1970), and <strong>David Norman Clark</strong> Docherty (and, through David, <strong>Levi Jesse Docherty</strong>). The brothers and sisters of the Alberta generations are drawn out in full in the family appendix below.</em></p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-the-brothers-the-sisters-and-the-alix-household"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Appendix: The wider family</span>The brothers, the sisters, and the Alix household</h2><p>For honesty&rsquo;s sake, this story has followed a single thread &mdash; one child in each generation, the one through whom the line came down. But no family is a single thread, and the people in it were never only &ldquo;heirs.&rdquo; They were brothers and sisters in loud, crowded prairie households. Here, at last, the record is warm enough &mdash; living memory, funeral cards, a McIver family bible, and a typed descendant report kept by Thomas&rsquo;s uncle David &mdash; that we can stop naming only the one who carried the line and start naming the whole table.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_3853-scaled.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="Thomas Richard Docherty with his sons Rick and Roy, about 1953" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Grampa Tom with Uncle Rick and Uncle Roy, about 1953.</figcaption></figure><div class="heritage-longread__gallery"><p class="heritage-longread__gallery-title">The family album &amp; bible</p><div class="heritage-longread__gallery-grid"><figure class="heritage-line__figure"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_3734-scaled.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="Elizabeth Maryanne with her brother David" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Mom and Uncle Dave.</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_3732-scaled.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="Uncle Roy and David Docherty" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Uncle Roy and David.</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_3738-scaled.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="Thomas Richard “Grampa Tom” Docherty with his owl" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Grampa Tom and his owl.</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_3739-scaled.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="Grampa Tom’s owl, a family keepsake" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The owl itself.</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--rotated"><span class="heritage-longread__rot heritage-longread__rot--ccw"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_4534.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="The Christian Herald Holy Bible, Red Letter Edition" loading="lazy" /></span><figcaption>The family bible &mdash; a Christian Herald &ldquo;Red Letter&rdquo; edition.</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_4543.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="A register page from the family bible" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The bible&rsquo;s register &mdash; Kroeger and Sullivan deaths at Coal Valley, Illinois.</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--rotated"><span class="heritage-longread__rot heritage-longread__rot--ccw"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/IMG_4538.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="Another register page from the family bible" loading="lazy" /></span><figcaption>More of the register, in the family&rsquo;s own hand.</figcaption></figure></div></div><p>There are two households here. The first is the blended household at <strong>Alix</strong> that Thomas Richard grew up in. The second is the Calgary-and-Edmonton household that he and Elizabeth Annie raised a generation later.</p><p><strong>The Alix household.</strong> When Augusta Nitz married Robert Docherty she did not come alone: she brought five children from her first marriage to Warren Martin Sullivan &mdash; <strong>Warren Alfred, Martin Herman, Earl William, Emma Lovina, and Lena Elizabeth Sullivan</strong> &mdash; so the Docherty children grew up among Sullivan half-siblings, an ordinary thing in a country built by people starting over. Robert and Augusta &mdash; who married in <strong>Aledo, Mercer County, Illinois, in 1908</strong> (see Chapter Five) &mdash; then had <strong>four</strong> children of their own, now confirmed from the family tree: a daughter, <strong>Lena</strong>; a daughter, <strong>Catharina Grace</strong>, born in Illinois in 1909; <strong>Thomas Richard</strong> (1914), through whom this line descends; and <strong>Abraham Lincoln Docherty</strong> (1917).</p><p><strong>Abraham Lincoln &ldquo;Abe&rdquo; Docherty.</strong> Thomas Richard&rsquo;s brother kept to the Alix country his whole life. He married <strong>Marjorie Evelyn Berg</strong> in 1944 and farmed in the parkland around <strong>Alix and Mirror</strong>. Abe died in 1986; Marjorie lived until 2008, and her funeral was held at <strong>Alix United Church</strong> &mdash; the same small prairie town where Robert had set the family down nearly a century before, still holding Dochertys three generations on. Abe and Marjorie&rsquo;s children &mdash; Thomas Richard&rsquo;s nieces and nephews, and Elizabeth Maryanne&rsquo;s first cousins on the Docherty side &mdash; were <strong>Rob, Allan, Brenda, Lorne, Catherine &ldquo;Cathy&rdquo;</strong> (who married Lorne Conklin), <strong>and Velma</strong>, settling in time across Stettler, Erskine, Mirror, Leduc, and into British Columbia. Through Abe, the Docherty name fanned out across central Alberta, where it lives yet.</p><p class="heritage-longread__aside"><em>(Two fainter Docherty echoes belong here too. A margin note in the family bible records that an &ldquo;Uncle Jim Docherty died in March 1938&rdquo; &mdash; most likely one of Robert&rsquo;s own brothers, and so a great-uncle to this generation. And far to the south are the Mercer County, Illinois Dochertys, the American cousins whose exact doorway into this family is still unclear. Both are taken up in the <a href="#lr-notes">Notes</a>.)</em></p><p><strong>The Calgary household.</strong> Thomas Richard and Elizabeth Annie raised four children, the family clustered in the Calgary years and later reaching to Edmonton: <strong>Donald Richard, Roy Thomas, David Norman Clark,</strong> and <strong>Elizabeth Maryanne</strong> Docherty.</p><p><strong>Donald Richard &mdash; &ldquo;Uncle Rick.&rdquo;</strong> The eldest, born in 1946. Richard became Rick, and Uncle Rick he has stayed. He married <strong>Elizabeth Ann Yaremichuk</strong> in 1973, at St Mark&rsquo;s Anglican in Edmonton, and the family records carry a later marriage, to a <strong>Carol</strong>, in 1984. He has children of his own &mdash; a small Docherty cousin-branch the documents on hand do not yet name, and that only the living family can fill in.</p><p><strong>Roy Thomas &mdash; the brother who was lost young.</strong> Born in 1951, Roy died on the 30th of August 1970, in Calgary, at only eighteen &mdash; the grief at the center of this generation, a loss his brothers and sister carried for the rest of their lives. The family&rsquo;s own records hold a tender detail: that Roy came to Thomas Richard and Elizabeth Annie as an adopted infant &mdash; chosen, not born, into the family, and loved as fully as any child of the blood. <em>(This rests on a secondary family data sheet and is set down here gently, flagged for the family&rsquo;s own confirmation.)</em></p><p><strong>David Norman Clark &mdash; &ldquo;Uncle David.&rdquo;</strong> Born in 1958. He married <strong>Laura Elaine Knutson</strong> in 1983, at Kelowna, and the Docherty line stepped west into British Columbia with them. Their son, <strong>Levi Jesse Docherty</strong>, born in 1984, is Thomas&rsquo;s first cousin &mdash; and, in his generation, one of the few to carry the Docherty surname itself forward.</p><p>And the youngest of the four was <strong>Elizabeth Maryanne</strong>, born in 1959 &mdash; Thomas&rsquo;s mother, the one through whom the line comes down to the present (Chapter Seven).</p><p>One boundary is worth marking, because it keeps the genealogy honest. The aunts and uncles Thomas knew on this side of the family were not all Dochertys. His mother&rsquo;s <em>mother</em>, Elizabeth Annie, came from the Hebridean <strong>McIver</strong> world &mdash; and the great-aunts and great-uncles who loom large in family memory, <strong>Eleanor</strong> (who married George Golden) and <strong>Catherine &ldquo;Cathy&rdquo;</strong> (who married Frank Wilson), with their many Golden, Wilson, Mulvey, Simmons, and Harris cousins, belong to <em>that</em> line, not the Docherty one. They have their due in the McIver, Campbell, and Cameron story. Here we keep to the Docherty name.</p><div class="heritage-longread__table-wrap"><table class="heritage-longread__table">
