<summary>Notes on the records</summary>
<div class="heritage-longread__notes-body">
<p>This appendix records what stands on proof and what stands on the family&rsquo;s own tree. The Docherty line is the <strong>thinnest-documented</strong> of the major lines in this family &mdash; not through carelessness but because it was, for most of its length, a poor working family of exactly the kind the record preserved worst. The shape of the story is secure; many individual links are not yet primary-confirmed.</p><h3>Key records still needed</h3><p>In rough order of value:</p><ol>
<li><strong>John Edward Docherty&rsquo;s death certificate</strong> &mdash; Johnstone, Renfrewshire, 1862. Names his parents; the single document most likely to crack the Irish-origin question.</li>
<li><strong>Peter Dougharty&rsquo;s death certificate</strong> &mdash; Hamilton, Lanarkshire, 1865. Names his parents; independently tests the John Edward → Peter link.</li>
<li><strong>Robert Docherty&rsquo;s Alberta homestead file</strong> + the <strong>LAC Western Land Grants</strong> database &mdash; arrival date, exact land, prior residence, naturalisation.</li>
<li><strong>Glasgow / Atlantic passenger lists</strong> (c. 1895&ndash;1910) and the <strong>1901 / 1911 / 1921 Canadian censuses</strong> for Robert.</li>
<li><strong>The Robert&ndash;Augusta marriage record</strong> (Aledo, Illinois, 1908) and Robert&rsquo;s *<em>1959 Calgary death record + </em>Calgary Herald<em> obituary</em>*.</li>
</ol><p>Both Scottish certificates exist in principle at ScotlandsPeople for a per-record fee (death certificates from 1856 on name <em>both parents</em> of the deceased). Until they are read, generations 1&ndash;3 stand as <em>probable / inherited</em>, not <em>verified</em>.</p><h3>Notes</h3><p><strong>Origins &mdash; a floor, not a fact.</strong> John &amp; Mary appear in the tree as John Edward&rsquo;s parents, undated and undocumented; treat them as the family&rsquo;s best inherited guess, possibly a placeholder &mdash; not a documented couple. The <strong>Donegal / Inishowen origin</strong> is a surname inference (Ó Dochartaigh is overwhelmingly a Donegal clan name) &mdash; strong but unproven, and complicated by the wife&rsquo;s surname <strong>McGeoghan</strong> (an Irish <em>midlands</em> name), so the family may join two corners of Ireland in Scotland. No Irish baptism will be found online: John Edward (1780) and Peter (~1801) predate every digitised Donegal register by thirty to eighty years. Tithe Applotment books (1823&ndash;37) and Griffith&rsquo;s Valuation (Donegal, 1857) are the realistic tools for mapping surviving kin, not for finding these specific men.</p><p><strong>On religion &mdash; a correction.</strong> An earlier draft assumed an <em>Irish Catholic</em> identity (a Mass rock on the Donegal hill, a chapel built of pennies). That was an inference from the Donegal surname, never a documented fact, and has been removed. Family testimony together with a primary artifact &mdash; the Protestant <em>Christian Herald</em> bible Robert&rsquo;s mother gave him on 30 May 1908 &mdash; establish a <strong>Protestant</strong> family from at least Robert&rsquo;s generation. A Protestant family with a Gaelic Donegal surname most plausibly traces to Ulster Protestant stock, though whether the line was <em>always</em> Protestant or descends from Gaelic Catholics who later conformed is unknown. The lesson stands for the whole project: a reasonable hypothesis (Donegal surname ⇒ likely Catholic) was allowed to harden into a theme before evidence confirmed it; the evidence, when it came, said otherwise.</p><p><strong>Spelling.</strong> Docherty, Dougharty, Doherty, Dougherty all appear for the same family &mdash; clerks transcribing an Irish name by ear. Normal for Irish-Scottish families, and not evidence of separate families; but any future search must run all spellings.</p><p><strong>The Illinois waystation &mdash; a migration correction.</strong> The emigration did not run straight from Scotland to Alberta but <strong>Scotland → Mercer County, Illinois → Alberta.</strong> Robert&rsquo;s father <strong>Thomas Dougharty</strong> took the family across the Atlantic in the 1890s and died in Mercer County in 1899; Robert appears in <strong>Pre-emption Township</strong> in the 1900 and 1910 US censuses, married <strong>Augusta Nitz at Aledo on 11 July 1908</strong>, and the blended household immigrated to Canada in <strong>1912</strong> (living around Medicine Hat, Red Deer, Lamerton, and Camrose County before Alix). The &ldquo;Mercer County Dochertys&rdquo; once thought a distant branch &mdash; Thomas B., John Kenneth, Jean Alice Liebendorfer, James Henderson &mdash; were the family&rsquo;s <em>own</em> people. The <em>1882 History of Mercer County</em> has <strong>zero</strong> Docherty hits in any spelling, so the family arrived after 1882 or below the historians&rsquo; notice; US censuses (1880/1900/1910) and Illinois marriage and naturalisation records are the way in.</p><p><strong>The wives were Scottish-born.</strong> Both <strong>Mary (Nancy) McGeoghan</strong> (b. 1786, Renfrewshire) and <strong>Mary Catherine</strong> (b. 8 Nov 1801, Govan, Glasgow) were Scottish-born; the Irish origin runs through the <strong>male line alone</strong> &mdash; John Edward and Peter. An earlier draft wrongly called Mary Catherine &ldquo;Irish-born&rdquo; &mdash; corrected in Chapter Three.</p><p><strong>The Nitz / Sullivan thread</strong> is the best-documented early part, because it is American. The Nitz and Kroeger families are well-attested in Rock Island County (German immigrants from West Prussia and Schleswig-Holstein); Augusta&rsquo;s first marriage to <strong>Warren Martin Sullivan</strong> (Moline, 1894) and her Sullivan children are traceable. Loose end: an earlier record puts Warren Sullivan&rsquo;s death in <strong>1910</strong>, which cannot stand against the confirmed <strong>1908</strong> remarriage &mdash; one date is wrong and needs rechecking.</p><p><strong>The &ldquo;twins&rdquo; question.</strong> The tree suggests Peter (b. ~1801) and <strong>Patrick Andrew Docherty</strong> (b. ~1801, d. 1871) were twin brothers &mdash; unconfirmed, resting on the matching birth year alone.</p><p><strong>Where the line becomes solid.</strong> From <strong>Thomas Richard Docherty (b. 1914, Alix)</strong> onward the story is in the recoverable Alberta record and living memory; those generations are <em>verified / living memory</em>, in contrast to the <em>probable / inherited</em> generations above.</p><p><strong>The living generations &mdash; sources, and one tender flag.</strong> The family appendix rests on living memory and on records photographed by Thomas&rsquo;s uncle <strong>David Norman Clark Docherty</strong> in 2026 &mdash; a McIver family bible, a typed <em>&ldquo;Descendants of Neill McIver&rdquo;</em> report, funeral cards, and data sheets from &ldquo;Aunty Eleanor&rdquo; (Eleanor Golden) and &ldquo;Aunt Bessie.&rdquo; Abe Docherty&rsquo;s family is confirmed by the obituary of his wife <strong>Marjorie (Berg) Docherty</strong> (d. 2008). Robert and Augusta&rsquo;s four biological children &mdash; <strong>Lena, Catharina Grace (b. 1909), Thomas Richard (1914), Abraham Lincoln (1917)</strong> &mdash; are confirmed from the tree (settling that Catharina Grace was a Docherty, not one of Augusta&rsquo;s Sullivan children). Left deliberately <em>un</em>-hardened: the note that <strong>Roy Thomas was adopted</strong> as an infant (a data sheet gives a birth name, &ldquo;Roy Murray Baker,&rdquo; and a Calgary adoption) rests on a single secondary source and is flagged for the family to confirm. One date is also still open: <strong>Thomas Cheesman&rsquo;s own birth year</strong> is recorded as both <strong>1980 and 1981</strong> (the Lineage table carries it as &ldquo;1980/81&rdquo;); which is correct has not yet been settled.</p><p><strong>On the McIver / Campbell / Cameron in-marriage.</strong> Elizabeth Annie McIver carries the Hebridean lines (McIver of Lewis, Campbell of South Uist, Cameron of Moray) and their own well-documented history &mdash; including the verified Great War death of <strong>Norman George McIver</strong> (46th Bn CEF, killed 3 June 1917, buried at Villers Station Cemetery, Pas-de-Calais, France). They have their own full telling and are summarised, not retold, here. Two tree-builder errors to avoid: Norman George did <em>not</em> die at &ldquo;Vimy Ridge, Belgium&rdquo; (it was France), and John Alexander McIver did <em>not</em> die at Saltcoats in 1940 (he died in Edmonton in 1970).</p><p><strong>Records update &mdash; since this draft.</strong> Independent records on FamilySearch have corroborated the Illinois years and the 1912 crossing for Robert and Augusta &mdash; including <strong>Catharina Grace&rsquo;s 1909 Illinois birth</strong> &mdash; moving the fifth generation from <em>probable</em> to <em>verified</em>. The two Scottish death certificates (John Edward, Johnstone 1862; Peter, Hamilton 1865) remain the pivotal documents still awaiting retrieval at ScotlandsPeople.</p><p class="heritage-longread__aside"><em>In short: believe the shape of it, and check the stones. The Docherty story is true in its shape &mdash; Donegal to Lanarkshire to Alberta &mdash; but not yet proven in many of its links. The path from &ldquo;probable&rdquo; to &ldquo;verified&rdquo; runs, above all, through two small Scottish death certificates and one Alberta homestead file. They are waiting to be read.</em></p>
</div>
</details>
